From a50af346ab7dfa66fe1bfd063078f4cd6775de8f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 23:43:45 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(gemm): vocabulary projection over exact 12-bit packed
 BF16 weights

Upstream-base: f8069b2c0be1311df3b112591c6b8876a843f8be

A BF16 vocabulary head is read once per decode step, so its cost is its
bytes. Each value keeps its sign and mantissa in one byte and its exponent
in a 4-bit code over a 15-exponent window chosen per tensor; code 0 keeps
exponent 0, so zeros and subnormals are exact. Values outside the window
are stored exactly in a per-row exception list and added after the main
dot product. pack() verifies that every bit round-trips and refuses
otherwise, so the packed tensor is the checkpoint tensor in 75% of the
bytes.

The Triton projection decodes in registers and accumulates in FP32 on
tensor cores. Each output sums its row's 128-column groups in order and
then its exceptions, independent of token count and tile configuration,
so a token's logits are the same bits in any batch.

On GB10, for DeepSeek V4.1's 32,320 x 5,120 TP4 vocabulary shard (25,992
exceptions), it reads 224-242 GB/s: 1.03-1.11 ms for 1-48 rows against
1.36-1.55 ms for cuBLAS BF16 and 1.31 ms for the BF16 row kernel at one
row.

Quality: lossless storage; logits differ from cuBLAS BF16 only by FP32
accumulation order. Tests: exact round trip including zeros, subnormals,
infinities and out-of-window values; reference match; bit-identical
logits across batch sizes and every tile configuration; CUDA graph
replay; shared-memory model against compiler limits; registry contract.
Upstream-status: local experiment, not submitted.
